      <![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><blockquote>my note: have read 2 of these. next: the one on sea power. library has it.</blockquote><hr/><h3>A grand-strategy reading list for the rest of us</h3><div>The Strategic Brief (newsletter), April 2026</div><div><br/></div><div>People keep asking for "one book on grand strategy". There isn't one. Here is a short path instead, ordered so each book makes the next easier.</div><div><br/></div><ol><li><b>A classic on war and politics</b> — read the first book and the chapter on the trinity of passion, chance and reason. Skip the rest on a first pass.</li><li><b>A history of the Peloponnesian War</b> — the Melian dialogue and the Sicilian expedition are the parts everyone cites; read them in context.</li><li><b>A modern account of sea power and trade routes</b> — why geography still decides which countries can project force.</li><li><b>A study of the Cold War's long containment strategy</b> — strategy as decades of patient, boring consistency.</li><li><b>A recent book on US–China competition</b> — read it last, so you can tell which parts are new and which are old ideas in new clothes.</li></ol><div><br/></div><div>The common thread: strategy is the art of matching limited means to large ends over time. Most failures in these books come from ends growing while means stay the same.</div></en-note>]]>
    </content>
  </note>
</en-export>
